---
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro'
import AssertionCard from '../../components/AssertionCard.astro'
import ChartFigure from '../../components/ChartFigure.astro'
import { assertion } from '../../lib/feeds'
import { bar } from '../../lib/chart-spec'

const toll = assertion('five-hundred-and-seventeen-residents-died')
const rank = assertion('sixteenth-of-eighty-eight-on-covid-deaths')
const winter = assertion('the-second-winter-killed-more-than-the-first')
const after = assertion('only-forty-two-deaths-after-february-2022')
const share = assertion('covid-was-a-sixth-of-the-countys-deaths')
const fall = assertion('the-fall-in-deaths-is-the-fall-in-covid')
const old = assertion('eighty-three-per-cent-of-the-dead-were-sixty-or-over')
const ladder = assertion('one-in-ten-over-eighty-and-one-in-five-thousand-under-twenty')
const sexes = assertion('more-women-caught-it-and-more-men-died')
const cases = assertion('thirty-four-thousand-cases-and-two-thousand-admissions')
const hosp = assertion('fifth-of-eighty-eight-on-hospitalisations')
const fifteen = assertion('one-case-in-fifteen-went-to-hospital')
const biggest = assertion('the-biggest-month-was-not-the-worst')
const undated = assertion('two-hundred-and-ninety-two-admissions-with-no-date')
const here = assertion('eight-hundred-and-fifteen-died-here-five-hundred-and-thirteen-lived-here')
const ratio = assertion('first-of-eighty-two-on-the-ratio')
const neighbours = assertion('four-of-five-neighbours-run-the-other-way')
const stateline = assertion('at-the-state-line-the-two-counts-agree')
const allcause = assertion('the-county-carries-a-third-more-dying-than-its-people-do')
const suppressed = assertion('ninety-six-of-a-hundred-and-seventy-weeks-suppressed')
const vax = assertion('forty-five-per-cent-completed-a-course')
const byage = assertion('eighty-two-per-cent-of-the-old-and-sixteen-of-the-young')
const spread = assertion('ohios-counties-run-from-nineteen-to-seventy-nine')
const booster = assertion('half-the-vaccinated-took-a-booster')
const zips = assertion('limas-codes-ran-half-again-blufftons')
const zipcheck = assertion('the-zip-file-and-the-county-file-agree')
const zipdenom = assertion('the-states-zip-populations-are-not-the-censuss')

const yearsSpec = bar(
  toll.figures.map((f) => f.label),
  toll.figures.map((f) => f.value),
  { yTitle: 'Deaths of county residents', labelValues: true },
)
const ladderSpec = bar(
  ladder.figures.map((f) => f.label),
  ladder.figures.map((f) => f.value),
  { yTitle: 'Per cent of the infected who died', tickSuffix: '%', labelValues: true },
)
const ratioSpec = bar(
  ratio.figures.map((f) => f.label),
  ratio.figures.map((f) => f.value),
  { yTitle: 'Deaths here per death of a resident', labelValues: true },
)
const vaxSpec = bar(
  byage.figures.map((f) => f.label),
  byage.figures.map((f) => f.value),
  { yTitle: 'Per cent completing a course', tickSuffix: '%', labelValues: true },
)
---

<Article slug="whose-deaths-these-were">
  <p>
    Ohio published its pandemic as two files. One counts a death against the county the person
    lived in; the other counts it against the county the death happened in. For most places the two
    are nearly the same number. For Allen County they are 513 and 815, and the difference between
    them is the largest of any county in the state.
  </p>

  <h2>Five hundred and seventeen</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={toll} open />
  <ChartFigure
    id="years"
    title="COVID-19 deaths of Allen County residents"
    caption="From the first death in March 2020 to the last the state recorded, on 24 August 2023."
    columns={['Year', 'Deaths']}
    rows={toll.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value.toLocaleString()])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="years" set:html={JSON.stringify(yearsSpec)} is:inline />
  <AssertionCard assertion={rank} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={winter} />
  <p>
    The county's worst month was December 2020 and its second worst was January 2022, thirteen
    months later, and between them stands a summer in which four people died. A pandemic told as one
    curve is two curves in this county, and the second is the taller.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={after} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={share} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={fall} />
  <p>
    The county's own death counts had already been read here as a spike that rose in 2021 and
    subsided by 2024, with the cause unstated because nothing on hand carried one. The cause is
    now on hand, and it accounts for almost the whole of the subsidence.
  </p>

  <h2>Who died</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={old} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={ladder} open />
  <ChartFigure
    id="ladder"
    title="Share of infected Allen County residents who died, by age"
    caption="Eight bands. Nobody in this county between twenty and thirty died of it."
    columns={['Age band', 'Per cent']}
    rows={ladder.figures.map((f) => [f.label, `${f.value.toFixed(2)}%`])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="ladder" set:html={JSON.stringify(ladderSpec)} is:inline />
  <AssertionCard assertion={sexes} />
  <p>
    Women were the majority of the infected and the minority of the dead. That is the pattern
    everywhere the disease was counted by sex, and it is here too, on a county of a hundred
    thousand people.
  </p>

  <h2>Cases and admissions</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={cases} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={hosp} open />
  <AssertionCard assertion={fifteen} />
  <p>
    Fifth of eighty-eight on hospitalisations and thirteenth on cases is a gap that wants an
    explanation, and there are two available. One is that people here were sicker. The other is that
    a county whose admissions run through two hospitals in one city reports them more completely
    than a county whose residents scatter across three. Nothing in the state's file separates them,
    and the node says so rather than choosing.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={biggest} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={undated} />

  <h2>Whose deaths these were</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={here} open />
  <ChartFigure
    id="ratio"
    title="Deaths occurring in the county per death of a resident"
    caption="The five highest of Ohio's eighty-two counties with a hundred or more resident deaths. Three of the other four hold Columbus, Toledo and Cincinnati."
    columns={['County', 'Ratio']}
    rows={ratio.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value.toFixed(3)])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="ratio" set:html={JSON.stringify(ratioSpec)} is:inline />
  <AssertionCard assertion={ratio} />
  <p>
    A county of a hundred thousand people tops a list whose next four entries are the state's
    medical centres. That is a measurement of a hospital's catchment, taken from death certificates
    — and the other end of it is visible next door.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={neighbours} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={stateline} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={allcause} />
  <p>
    The same gap sits in the county's ordinary deaths, at about a third rather than three fifths.
    Roughly 1,740 people a year die inside Allen County and roughly 1,300 of them are its own. Both
    figures are wanted, and neither of them is the county's death toll without the word that says
    which.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={suppressed} />

  <h2>Vaccination</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={vax} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={byage} open />
  <ChartFigure
    id="vax"
    title="Share completing a primary course in Allen County, by age"
    caption="On 10 May 2023, the last day federal county reporting existed."
    columns={['Group', 'Per cent']}
    rows={byage.figures.map((f) => [f.label, `${f.value.toFixed(1)}%`])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="vax" set:html={JSON.stringify(vaxSpec)} is:inline />
  <AssertionCard assertion={spread} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={booster} />

  <h2>The one map the state drew</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={zips} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={zipcheck} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={zipdenom} />
  <p>
    Cases by postal code is the only sub-county measure Ohio published, and it comes with its own
    population column that disagrees with the census by up to seven and a half per cent. Read as an
    ordering it is sound; read as levels it carries a denominator nobody stated. Bluffton at the
    bottom and Lima at the top is the ordering, and what separates them is not in the file.
  </p>
</Article>
